.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH FSEEK 3S
.SH NAME
fseek, ftell, rewind, fgetpos, fsetpos \- reposition a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int ptrname);
.PP
.B long ftell(FILE *stream);
.PP
.B void rewind(FILE *stream);
.PP
.B int fgetpos(FILE *stream, fpos_t *pos);
.PP
.B int fsetpos(FILE *stream, const fpos_t *pos);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fseek
sets the position of the next input or output
operation on the
.IR stream .
The new position is at the signed distance
.I offset
bytes
from the beginning, the current position, or the end of the file,
according as
.I ptrname
has the value 0, 1, or 2.
.B Note:
SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR and SEEK_END are those three values, and <stdio.h> defines
them; v7 wrote the digits.
.PP
.I Fseek
undoes any effects of
.IR  ungetc (3).
.PP
.I Ftell
returns the current value of the offset relative to the beginning
of the file associated with the named
.IR stream .
It is measured in bytes on UNIX;
on some other systems it is a magic cookie,
and the only foolproof way to obtain an
.I offset
for
.IR fseek .
.PP
.I Rewind(stream)
is equivalent to
.I "fseek(stream, 0L, 0)."
.PP
.B Note:
.I fgetpos
and
.I fsetpos
are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.  They save and restore a position through an
.BR fpos_t ,
which is the portable spelling of the same thing:
.I fgetpos
records where the stream stands and
.I fsetpos
puts it back.  Each returns 0 on success and a non-zero value on failure.
.B Fpos_t is an int here
\- one word, the same
.B off_t
the kernel counts bytes in \- because v7 had no such type and used a
.B long
for a file offset, which is also one word.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
lseek(2),
fopen(3),
stdio(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fseek
returns \-1 for improper seeks.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
An offset is
.B one word,
not the PDP-11's pair.
.B long
is
.B int
here, and so is
.BR off_t ,
which is why
.IR lseek (2)
lost an argument that v7's gate carried.
.PP
One v7 bug is fixed here rather than carried:
.I fseek
left its resolved position uninitialised on a path that could reach the return,
so a seek that took that path reported a position that was never computed.  It
is initialised to \-1 now.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/fseek.c, ftell.c, rew.c, fgetpos.c, fsetpos.c
